# Old domain → primary (frotzsmith.frotzinc.com). Netlify serves domain
# aliases as-is unless told otherwise; these must come first so they win
# over every other rule, including Nitro's appended catch-all. `!` forces
# the redirect even when a file exists at the path.
# Except sw.js: browsers that visited the old domain still run its service
# worker, which serves pages from cache and never reaches the 301. Their
# update check fetches sw.js; a cross-origin redirect fails it, so the old
# domain serves a worker that unregisters itself instead (public/sw-retire.js).
https://frotzsmith.com/sw.js      /sw-retire.js  200!
https://www.frotzsmith.com/sw.js  /sw-retire.js  200!
https://frotzsmith.com/*      https://frotzsmith.frotzinc.com/:splat  301!
https://www.frotzsmith.com/*  https://frotzsmith.frotzinc.com/:splat  301!

# Plausible analytics, first-party. The tracker script itself is VENDORED
# (public/pa/script.js, reviewed, sha256 recorded in tools/vendored-files.json),
# so no third-party code runs here; only the event beacon is proxied. These live in _redirects (not
# netlify.toml [[redirects]]): on Netlify the build runs Nitro's netlify
# preset, which emits its own _redirects catch-all — FILE rules are honored
# and processed in order, while every toml redirect is shadowed by it
# (verified live 2026-07-09: toml rules never matched; toml HEADERS work).
# Nitro appends its catch-all after these lines, so first-match wins here.
/pa/event      https://plausible.io/api/event  200
